'''Coccola dels distribuïdors Abyala, S.A.''' (CdDA) is the largest [[Coccola]] bottling and distribution company in the [[Abyala]]n market and one of the largest beverage bottlers in the world by volume. The company holds exclusive bottling franchises from [[HAWT Inc.]] for seven countries — [[Potocsí]], [[Balisca]], [[Tapuya]], [[Asikyira]], [[Lacashe]], [[Sanrobonia]], and [[Balboa]] — covering a combined population of over 500 million people. It operates 20 bottling plants and a distribution network of approximately 2.6 million retail outlets across its territory. It is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[Sanropura Stock Exchange]], under the ticker CDDA on both exchanges.

CdDA was formed in 2006 through the merger of '''Coccola dari Sanrobonia''', the HAWT Inc. franchisee for Sanrobonia and Balboa, and '''Abyala Coccola Corporació''', the franchisee for Potocsí, Balisca, Tapuya, Asikyira, and Lacashe. The merger created a unified bottler covering the entire Abyalan market, with the combined entity inheriting both predecessor companies' stock exchange listings. CdDA is the largest beverage company in Abyala by revenue and the largest company listed on the Sanropura Stock Exchange by market capitalisation. Its headquarters are in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], reflecting the city's position as the principal financial hub for the Abyalan region and the historical base of Abyala Coccola Corporació.

== History ==

=== Predecessor companies ===

==== Coccola dari Sanrobonia (1962–2006) ====

Coccola dari Sanrobonia was established in 1962 by [[Sanusi Rahman]], a Sanrobonian businessman who had built a fortune in the import and distribution of consumer goods across the Sanrobonian market. Rahman recognised that the growing Sanrobonian middle class represented a significant untapped market for [[Coccola]], which was then available only through expensive imports from [[Okaiken]] and had no local bottling infrastructure.

Rahman negotiated with [[HAWT Inc.]], the Okami-based food and beverage conglomerate, for the exclusive bottling franchise for Sanrobonia and Balboa. The franchise agreement granted the company the exclusive right to produce, bottle, and distribute Coccola-branded products in those two markets, in exchange for a royalty on sales paid to HAWT Inc. HAWT Inc. took a 35 per cent equity stake to align the bottler's interests with the brand owner.

The company's first bottling plant opened in Sanropura in 1963, followed by a second plant in Tanjung Seroja (1971) and a third in Makassar (1985). A fourth Sanrobonian plant was opened in Banjarmasin in 1995. A Balboan plant was opened in Sant Cristòfor in 1988 to serve the Balboan market. Sanrobonia became the company's largest and most important market, driven by the country's large population and growing consumer economy. The company was listed on the [[Sanropura Stock Exchange]] in 1985, with the Rahman family retaining a 25 per cent stake and HAWT Inc. retaining its 35 per cent stake.

By 2005, Coccola dari Sanrobonia operated five bottling plants, employed approximately 14,000 people, and generated annual revenue of $4.2 billion. It was the largest listed company on the Sanropura Stock Exchange.

==== Abyala Coccola Corporació (1965–2006) ====

Abyala Coccola Corporació was established in 1965 as a consortium of Potocsí and Balboan investors who secured the HAWT Inc. bottling franchise for the mainland Abyalan market — Potocsí, Balisca, Tapuya, Asikyira, and Lacashe. The company was originally headquartered in Port Soledat, Potocsí, reflecting the leading role of Potocsí capital in the consortium and the city's position as the principal commercial centre of mainland Abyala.

The company's first bottling plant opened in Port Soledat (Potocsí) in 1966, followed by Tapuya (1972), Asikyira (1978), and Lacashe (1983). In its early years, the company served the Baliscan market through imports of bottled Coccola produced at its Potocsí plants.

The Port Soledat bottling plant, opened in 1966, was expanded multiple times and became the largest Coccola production facility in Abyala. The company's Potocsí operations were disrupted during the [[Camboriú War]] with Balisca and the subsequent political instability. The company maintained operations throughout the conflict, and the Port Soledat plant became a symbol of economic continuity in the region. However, the war and its aftermath made Potocsí an increasingly difficult operating environment, and in 1979 the company relocated its corporate headquarters from Port Soledat to [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], citing the city's political stability, established financial infrastructure, and proximity to the mainland Abyalan market. The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1998, reflecting its new Balboan domicile and the growing importance of the mainland Abyalan market.

The Camboriú War had a lasting impact on the company's Baliscan operations. During the war, the cessation of trade between Potocsí and Balisca cut off all imports of bottled Coccola to the Baliscan market, creating a prolonged "soda shortage" that lasted well beyond the conflict itself, as diplomatic relations between the two countries were not restored until the [[Kuluba Accords]] of 1982. The shortage created an opening for local Baliscan beverage companies, which expanded rapidly to fill the gap in the market. By the time trade relations normalised in the 1980s, these domestic competitors had established strong brand loyalty and distribution networks, making it difficult for Coccola to re-enter the Baliscan market. The company was unable to open its first Baliscan bottling plant until 1993, when the Castejón facility was established as a {{w|worker cooperative}} under Balisca's cooperative land tenure system, as mandated by the Basic Law: Land. Additional cooperative plants followed in Alunzaro (1996) and Macaio (1999). Despite these investments, Coccola remained a smaller player in the Baliscan market compared to the domestic competitors that had emerged during the shortage, and Balisca consistently represented a smaller share of the company's revenue than its population would suggest.

By 2005, Abyala Coccola Corporació operated 11 bottling plants across five countries, employed approximately 22,000 people, and generated annual revenue of $7.8 billion. It was one of the largest companies listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor.

=== Merger (2006) ===

In 2004, HAWT Inc. initiated discussions with the boards of both Coccola dari Sanrobonia and Abyala Coccola Corporació, advocating for a merger of the two franchisees to create a unified bottler covering the entire Abyalan market. HAWT Inc.'s management argued that a single, larger bottler would achieve economies of scale in procurement, manufacturing, and distribution, and would be better positioned to invest in the infrastructure needed to serve a combined population of over 500 million people.

The merger was announced in March 2005 and completed on 1 January 2006, with the combined entity named Coccola dels distribuïdors Abyala, S.A. The merger was structured as a stock-for-stock transaction, with shareholders of Coccola dari Sanrobonia receiving 0.45 CdDA shares for each Sanrobonia share and shareholders of Abyala Coccola Corporació receiving 0.55 CdDA shares for each Corporació share. The Rahman family emerged as the largest individual non-HAWT shareholder, with an 11 per cent stake in the combined entity.

The merged company inherited both predecessor companies' stock exchange listings, becoming dual-listed on the Sanropura Stock Exchange and the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor. The headquarters were established in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], reflecting the city's position as the principal financial hub for the Abyalan region and the historical base of Abyala Coccola Corporació. Consistent with the practice at HAWT Inc.-affiliated bottlers, the merged entity was led by professional managers appointed from HAWT Inc.'s corporate ranks in Okaiken, rather than by representatives of the predecessor companies' founding families.

=== Post-merger (2006–present) ===

The post-merger period was marked by the integration of the two predecessor companies' operations, which took approximately three years to complete. The integration involved consolidating distribution networks, standardising bottling processes, and harmonising IT systems across the seven markets. The company also invested in new bottling plants, opening facilities in Sanrobonia (2010), Balboa (Port de Sant Cristòfor, 2011), Balisca (Pindorama, 2012), and Tapuya (2013), bringing the total number of plants to 20 by 2016. The new Baliscan plant at Pindorama was established as a worker cooperative under Baliscan law, consistent with the existing cooperative structure of the Baliscan bottling operations.

The company expanded its product portfolio beyond Coccola to include other HAWT Inc.-branded sparkling beverages, still beverages, and juices, all produced under the HAWT franchise agreement. The franchise was most recently renewed in 2022.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 had a significant impact on CdDA's operations, as the closure of restaurants, bars, and entertainment venues reduced on-premise consumption. However, the company's retail distribution network — which included supermarkets, convenience stores, and kiosks — allowed it to maintain sales through the pandemic, as consumers shifted to at-home consumption. The company also expanded its e-commerce and home delivery capabilities in response to the pandemic.

By 2026, CdDA operated 20 bottling plants across its seven markets, employed approximately 48,000 people, and distributed its products through approximately 2.6 million retail outlets. The company's revenue was $16.5 billion, and its market capitalisation was $22 billion, making it the largest company listed on the Sanropura Stock Exchange and one of the largest companies on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor.

== Operations ==

CdDA operates across two principal business segments:

* '''Bottling operations''': The company operates 20 bottling plants across its seven markets — Sanrobonia (5 plants), Potocsí (4 plants), Balisca (4 plants), Tapuya (2 plants), Balboa (2 plants), Asikyira (2 plants), and Lacashe (1 plant). The plants produce Coccola and other HAWT Inc.-branded beverages from concentrate supplied by HAWT Inc. The company's largest plant, in Port Soledat, has a production capacity of approximately 2.8 billion litres per year.
* '''Bottling operations in Balisca''': The 4 bottling plants in Balisca are not directly owned by CdDA. Under Balisca's cooperative land tenure system, as established by the Basic Law: Land, the bottling plants are owned and operated by worker cooperatives. These cooperatives produce Coccola and other HAWT Inc.-branded beverages under contract to CdDA, which supplies the concentrate and purchases the finished bottled product back from the cooperatives for distribution. The Castejón cooperative plant is the second-largest Coccola production facility in Abyala, with a capacity of 2.2 billion litres per year. This arrangement, unique among HAWT Inc.'s global bottling network, was negotiated as part of the franchise agreement for the Baliscan market and reflects the country's cooperative economic model.
* '''Distribution operations''': The company distributes its products through approximately 2.6 million retail outlets across its territory, ranging from large supermarkets and convenience stores in urban centres to small kiosks and street vendors in rural areas. The distribution network includes 380 refrigerated warehouses, a fleet of 12,000 delivery vehicles, and a network of approximately 8,500 small-scale distributors who serve remote areas. In Balisca, distribution is handled through cooperative distribution networks affiliated with the bottling cooperatives.

The company's product portfolio includes Coccola — the flagship brand — as well as other HAWT Inc.-branded sparkling beverages, still beverages, and juices. All products are produced under the HAWT franchise agreement, with HAWT Inc. supplying concentrate and owning the brand. Outside Balisca, CdDA handles production, bottling, and distribution directly. In Balisca, the worker cooperative bottling plants handle production under contract, while CdDA handles distribution.

Coccola is the market-leading cola brand in Sanrobonia, the company's largest market by revenue. In Potocsí and Balboa, Coccola is the second-best-selling cola brand, competing with [[Jalapen]] (owned by [[Expres Polla]]). In Balisca, despite the country's large population, Coccola holds a smaller market share than in other Abyalan markets, as local competitors that emerged during the Camboriú War soda shortage established strong positions before Coccola could re-enter the market. The company's markets by revenue are, in descending order: Sanrobonia, Potocsí, Balisca, Tapuya, Balboa, Asikyira, and Lacashe.

== Ownership ==

CdDA's ownership structure reflects the 2006 merger and the company's status as a HAWT Inc. franchisee:

* [[HAWT Inc.]]: 35 per cent — the brand owner and concentrate supplier, holding a significant equity stake to align the bottler's interests with the brand
* [[Rahman family]]: 11 per cent — descendants of Sanusi Rahman, the founder of Coccola dari Sanrobonia; passive shareholders with no management role
* [[Sanrobonia Sovereign Wealth Fund]]: 8 per cent — a sovereign wealth fund investment reflecting CdDA's status as Sanrobonia's largest listed company
* Public float: 46 per cent — traded on the Sanropura Stock Exchange and the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor

The company is professionally managed, with the chairman and CEO typically appointed from [[HAWT Inc.]]'s corporate ranks in [[Okaiken]] or recruited from other major corporations. The Rahman family, while remaining the largest individual non-HAWT shareholder, has not held a management position in the company since the 2006 merger.

